question:
Three circles are inscribed in a rectangle of width $w$ and height $h$, as shown. Two of the circles are congruent; one is tangent to the left and top sides, the other to the left and bottom sides, and they are tangent to each other. The third circle is larger and is tangent to the top, bottom and right sides of the rectangle and to both smaller circles. What is the ratio of $h$ to $w$? Express your answer as a decimal to the nearest hundredth.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real r=1,w=3+2*sqrt(2),h=4;
draw((0,0)--(w,0)--(w,h)--(0,h)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle((1,1),1));draw(circle((1,3),1));draw(circle((w-2,2),2));
draw((0,-0.45)--(w,-0.45),Arrows(4));label("$w$",(w/2,-0.45),S);
draw((w+0.45,0)--(w+0.45,h),Arrows(4));label("$h$",(w+0.45,h/2),E);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

solution:
Let the small radius be $r$. The two small circles are stacked along the left side, so $h=4r$, and the large circle touches the top and bottom, so its radius is $2r$. Put the lower small centre at $(r,r)$ and the large centre at $(x,2r)$.

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(5.5cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
real w=3+2*sqrt(2),h=4;pair P1=(1,1),P2=(1,3),Q=(w-2,2);
draw((0,0)--(w,0)--(w,h)--(0,h)--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
draw(circle(P1,1));draw(circle(P2,1));draw(circle(Q,2));
draw(P1--Q,blue);draw(P1--(Q.x,P1.y),dashed);draw(Q--(Q.x,P1.y),dashed);
draw(Q--(Q.x,4),dotted);draw(P1--(0,1),dotted);
dot(P1);dot(P2);dot(Q);
label("$r$",(0.5,1),N);label("$2r$",(Q.x,3),E);
label("$3r$",P1+0.75*(Q-P1),dir(110),blue);label("$r$",(Q.x,1.5),E);label("$d$",(2.65,1),S);
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

Tangency of the circles gives a centre distance of $r+2r=3r$, with vertical difference $r$, so the horizontal difference is $d=\sqrt{9r^2-r^2}=2\sqrt2\,r$. Hence $w=r+2\sqrt2\,r+2r=(3+2\sqrt2)r$ and
\[
\dfrac {h}{w}=\dfrac{4}{3+2\sqrt2}=4(3-2\sqrt2)=12-8\sqrt2\approx\boxed{0.69}.
\]

$20400=2^4\cdot3\cdot5^2\cdot17$. The digit $5$ is used once, so at most one of the numbers is divisible by $5$ (a number divisible by $5$ with no digit $0$ ends in $5$). Hence one number is divisible by $25$; with no $0$, $7$ available, it ends in $25$.
\begin{itemize}
\item If it is the two-digit $25$: the other two multiply to $816=2^4\cdot3\cdot17$, using digits $1,3,4,6$. The one-digit number is $1,3,4$ or $6$, leaving $816,272,204,136$ for the three-digit number, none of which uses the remaining digits.
\item If it is a three-digit $\underline{a}25$ with $a\in\{1,3,4,6\}$: $125$ and $625$ are powers of $5$ (too many factors of $5$) and $325=25\cdot13$ does not divide $20400$, so it is $425=25\cdot17$. The others multiply to $48$ using digits $1,3,6$: $3\cdot16=48$ works ($1\cdot36$ and $6\cdot13$ do not).
\end{itemize}
So the numbers are $3,16,425$, and their sum is $\boxed{444}$.

Let $AB=5$ and $AC=6$, with circumradius $R=5$. By the extended law of sines, $\sin C=\dfrac{AB}{2R}=\dfrac{1}{2}$ and $\sin B=\dfrac{AC}{2R}=\dfrac{3}{5}$. So $C=30^\circ$ or $150^\circ$, and $\cos B=\pm\dfrac{4}{5}$.

Since $C$ is opposite the shorter side $AB<AC$, $C<B$, so $C=30^\circ$ ($C=150^\circ$ would leave $B<30^\circ$). Two cases remain, $B$ acute or obtuse, and both occur:
\begin{center}

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.2cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);pair A=5*dir(90),B=5*dir(150),C=5*dir(90-2*aSin(0.6));
draw(circle(O,5),gray);draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(O);label("$O$",O,S);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,W);label("$C$",C,E);
label("$5$",(A+B)/2+1.15*dir(120));label("$6$",(A+C)/2,dir(50));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}
\qquad

\begin{asy}
usepackage("amsmath");
size(4.2cm);
defaultpen(fontsize(10pt));
pair O=(0,0);pair A=5*dir(90),B=5*dir(150),C=5*dir(90+2*aSin(0.6));
draw(circle(O,5),gray);draw(A--B--C--cycle,linewidth(0.9));
dot(O);label("$O$",O,S);
label("$A$",A,N);label("$B$",B,NW);label("$C$",C,W);
label("$5$",(A+B)/2+1.15*dir(120));label("$6$",(A+C)/2,dir(-30));
label("\mbox{}",(min(currentpicture,user=true)+max(currentpicture,user=true))/2,black); // keeps the text after the figure black when LaTeX typesets the labels inline
\end{asy}

\end{center}
The third side is $BC=2R\sin A=10\sin(B+C)=10(\sin B\cos C+\cos B\sin C)=10\left(\dfrac{3}{5}\cdot\dfrac{\sqrt3}{2}\pm\dfrac{4}{5}\cdot\dfrac{1}{2}\right)$:
\begin{itemize}
\item $B$ acute (left): $BC=3\sqrt3+4\approx9.20$;
\item $B$ obtuse (right): $BC=3\sqrt3-4\approx1.20$.
\end{itemize}
So the third side is $\boxed{3\sqrt3+4\text{ or }3\sqrt3-4}$.
